<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#haitian-creole">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haitian Creole</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kreyòl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haitian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> is a French-based creole language spoken by almost the whole population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. It developed in the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> from the eighteenth century among enslaved Africans of many languages, combining mostly French vocabulary with grammatical influences from West African languages. Long overshadowed by French, the language of the elite and the state, it became an official language alongside French under the constitution of 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-creole.jpg" alt="Haitian Creole" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fobos92 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idioma_haitiano.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nearly all speak <b>Haitian Creole</b>, French the educated 42%; 55% were Catholic and 29% Protestant in 2018, while 50–80% keep some element of Vodou, recognised by the state in 2003.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This produced a broad free peasantry, unusual in the plantation Americas, but it ended large-scale sugar exports, reduced state revenue and left a gulf between the rural majority, who spoke <b>Haitian Creole</b> and practised Vodou alongside Catholicism, and a French-speaking urban elite of officials, merchants and landowners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike Santería and Candomblé, whose liturgy is in Yoruba, Vodou’s liturgies are mainly in <b>Haitian Creole</b>, the everyday language of most Vodouists, with some songs in Fon or Yoruba.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>A <b>Haitian Creole</b> version, &quot;Desalinyèn&quot;, was made by Raymond A. Moyse, and the singer Ansy Dérose (1934–1998) helped make it popular in 1980.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-dessalinienne">La Dessalinienne</a></li></ul></section>
